A||||12||fiero56||For owners of RAM clutch sets with v6||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||03-18-2001||07:24 PM||||Does your clutch feel very "solid"? Meaning, does it feel like the clamp force is enough to handle the 2.8 when driven "spirited"? <P>Mine has always felt really soft, so a few weeks ago I tried a little test. I set the engine at 3,500rpm, side stepped the clutch and floored it. The clutch never mated to the flywheel and pressure plate. The engine just slowly wound down rpm until the clutch mated finally around 1,500 rpm. The tires NEVER EVEN chirped!!! I was laughing my a$$ off, that my '88 v-6 5-speed can't even break 'em loose off the line because of my wimpy clutch. The clutch set has 20K miles on it, and never slips once the clutch is mated. But it makes spirited driving a little less spirited. If the clutch is already mated to the flywheel, I can literally smoke them all the way through first(only done that twice, tires aren't cheap, and I'm not made of money <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">). If I am making a right or left turn, I can dump the clutch and rip 'em up through first, but never in a straight line by duming the clutch. <P>Any other RAM owners with the same problem? <P>------------------<BR>           <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pffsig1.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||88formula||03-18-2001||07:30 PM||||I don't have a Ram clutch but with my stock clutch and 48k miles I could rev up to 3000-3500 rpm, dump the clutch and burn the tires all the time.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||03-18-2001||10:36 PM||||Well, my RAM clutch only has about 400 miles on it, so I can't say for sure, but it feels tight now.  Whether or not it will stay that way, who knows?  I have chirped 2nd by accident though, just in spirited driving.  Haven't dumped the clutch cuz it doesn't have enough break in time, yet.||||reg||6
Z||000003||Greg Piet||03-19-2001||12:50 AM||||If you dump the clutch at 3500 and the tires dont break loose, something is wrong...<P>My stock clutch could even break racing tires loose without any problems.<P>I have done your test in my 4speed v6 and had no problem "lighting them up"  I just did your test when the clutch didnt quite feel right...  needless to say it behaved similarly to your results and I am replacing the clutch as of present.<P>I do not imagine a RAM brand clutch being worse as the friction materials are better and have more springs...  Was the flywheel resurfaced?<P>------------------<BR>SCCA Region 72<BR>#47 CSP<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.physics.unlv.edu/~gregpiet/Jan1617s.jpg">   <BR>Our lady of blessed acceleration dont fail me now!<p>[This message has been edited by Greg Piet (edited 03-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||03-19-2001||07:10 AM||||I replaced the flywheel when I replaced the clutch($250). <P>The clutch engages smoothly. No chattering, or anything like that. My v-6 4-speed with factory clutch has no problem lighting them up. But, it has a much heavier pedal feel also. <BR>I wonder if the p/p bolts have backed their way out some? Maybe I will pull the tranny sometime this week and take a peek.||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||03-19-2001||08:19 AM||||i had the SAME issue with my RAM set-up.. i replaced it with a Center force and then an ACT. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||03-19-2001||12:01 PM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 1FST2M6:<BR><B>i had the SAME issue with my RAM set-up... </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Glad to know I'm not the only one who has encountered this problem with the RAM clutch set. I'll keep it in there, and trash it when I finish the 2.8 in the garage.<P>BTW, Greg, those wheels looks very MR2ish. I like, what are they?<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 03-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Steven||03-19-2001||12:19 PM||||Those wheels look like the wheels that Dodge used to put on the Omni GLH, are they?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||03-19-2001||12:57 PM||||It's unlikely.  I'm pretty sure the Dodge Omni was a 4 bolt pattern.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Steven||03-19-2001||01:14 PM||||Omni's had 4 bolts, but the GLH which was a turbocharged deal had 5 bolts. I think they used them on the Shelby Chargers also.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Greg Piet||03-19-2001||03:48 PM||||heh...   dodge omni...   Your not too far off!<P>1984 or 85 Dodge charger Shelby (2.2litre turbo POS).<P>I have had them for about 12 years now...  I needed some rims and they were laying around.<P>Its amazing what mothers polish and sandpaper will do.<P>I am now concerned why the RAM set up would do that!  I was going to get the Kevlar RAM disc and pressure plate, and now I am scared to do that.||||reg||1
Z||000011||1FST2M6||03-19-2001||03:54 PM||||the pressure plate isn't strong enought to hold the torque... i put mine in with a BRAND NEW flywheel from the local GM dealer so there was a perfect matting surface. and the pressure plae just wouldn't hold it.. I'm sure the disc is fine if you get a decent pressure plate. NAPA part # CA1914... the stock unit has an average clamping force of 1900lbs.. the Napa unit claims 2100-2500. and having installed 2 (86GT and 87 4cyl) they seems just fine.. not a pain to drive and no dash shaking like GT Bastards SE  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  but most of that is the solid disc..<BR> <A HREF="http://www.clutchnet.com" TARGET=_blank>www.clutchnet.com</A>  and check out the kush-lok disc.||||reg||1
Z||000012||fiero56||03-19-2001||04:34 PM||||I don't have any problem with the disk either. I also installed a brand new GM flywheel, to make sure everything was perfect. The pressure plated clamp force is just not enough. It has such a light pedal feel it is very nice for daily driving, I really like that attribute since I do a lot of stop and go driving. Plus, it is one of the smoothest engaging clutches I'ver ever driven. But my daily driver is also my "fun weekend car", and my daily driver cluth setup just won't do fun stuff, it also makes any sort of speed shifing absolutely useless. If I can't chirp them in first, there's no way I can in second. I'd go for just the disk, and get a different plate, if I were to do it over again.||||reg||1
